<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/failure-fracture-and-fatigue">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Failure analysis is <strong>materials science\'s forensic department</strong>: why things
    break, when, and how to design so they don\'t. Two villains — stress-concentrating cracks
    and cycle-accumulating fatigue — explain most of it; both are detectable, both are
    designable-against, and both ignored have killed people.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="failure modes, Griffith, toughness, fatigue" widget="equationStep" config={fractureCfg} />

  <h2>Case files that built the discipline</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Comet disasters (1954):</strong> the first jet airliner broke apart in flight — pressurisation cycles fatigue-cracked the fuselage from window corners. The wrecks were rebuilt in water tanks and cycled to failure: fracture mechanics\'s founding case, and why airliner corners are now generously rounded.</li>
    <li><strong>Liberty ships (1943):</strong> welded hulls cracked in cold water, sometimes splitting in half — brittle transition temperature plus weld stress concentrations; the birth of "ductile–brittle transition temperature" as a design parameter.</li>
    <li><strong>Aloha Airlines (1988):</strong> 24 years of pressurisation cycles — multiple-site fatigue cracking joined into one catastrophic unzip; a flight attendant\'s heroic landing made it survivable and made aging-aircraft inspection mandatory (the NDT inspection disciplines: X-ray, ultrasonics, dye-penetrant — the spectroscopy lesson\'s instruments, listening for cracks).</li>
    <li><strong>De Havilland\'s lesson generalises:</strong> every modern design codes inspectability (can we find the crack?) alongside strength — the safety lesson\'s detectability factor, materialised.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Griffith (1920); ASM Handbook Vol. 11 (Failure Analysis); the NTSB/AAIB Comet and Aloha reports.</p>
</TopicPage>
